      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION AND OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Stenting has contributed to improve the early angiographic result, the restenosis rate and the problem of acute and subacute coronary occlusion. In spite of this, the restenosis phenomenon still remains a problem to be completely solved. The aim of the study was to identify clinical, angiographical and procedural factors that are predictive of in-stent restenosis after successful implantation of coronary stent."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "We retrospectively analyzed 202 lesions, in 176 consecutive patients who underwent stent implantation with success in our hospital between January 1995 and August 1998. All patients had a clinical follow-up and an angiography after six months of stent implantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "From 202 lesions evaluated, 47 evolved with restenosis (23%). The only independent predictive variables were: to be receiving hypolipemiant treatment before stenting (OR: 0.3; IC: 0.1-0.8), the use of high pressure for stent implantation (OR: 0.4; IC: 0.2-0.9), to implant stent in < 3.1 mm (OR: 2.2; IC: 1.1-4.5) and to have a residual stenosis > 30% after stenting (OR: 13; IC: 1.5-120)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The only statistical variables associated with in-stent restenosis phenomenon were: be under hypolipemiant treatment before the procedure and the use of high pressures for stent implantation; while risk factors arose: to implant stent in vessels < 3.1 mm and suboptimal angiography result after stenting."
        }
      ]},
